From 4450216c9e3269d98bfef663a1b11a0b0285fe71 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jasl <jasl9187@hotmail.com>
Date: Sun, 2 Aug 2026 03:55:43 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 194/194] cleanup(dsv4): skip the pool's Q buffer when Q is
 written in place

Follow-up to the 41-commit merge. Six candidate dead paths were audited for
reachability before touching anything, and FIVE came back live -- the useful
result of the audit was mostly what NOT to delete:

  - eager_scratch pool q_out / _q: live. padded_heads == n_local_heads is a
    property of ONE attention class, not an invariant. The FlashMLA class pads
    everything to 64, so at TP=2 (32 heads) it needs the buffer on every
    forward. Reachable via VLLM_DEEPSEEK_V4_FLASHINFER_SM120_DECODE=0, a
    flashinfer without trtllm_batch_decode_sparse_mla_dsv4, an explicit
    --attention-backend FLASHMLA_SPARSE_DSV4, or any non-SM12x device.
  - our _q_padded_scratch machinery from 53b6d1110c: live. It covers exactly
    the case upstream's pool punts on ("TODO: support dbo if needed") --
    ubatching, where the pool is None.
  - reserve_profile_scratch: live, and the obvious cleanup would have been a
    bug. The scratch cache is class-level and keyed without layer identity, so
    the MAIN layers' profile reservation is what pre-warms the buffer the DRAFT
    layers later consume. Adding "if pool is not None: return" would have moved
    that allocation past the profile run -- reintroducing the OOM jasl/vllm#26
    fixed.
  - XPU/AMD parallel implementations and the discarded-upstream residue in
    nvidia/dspark.py: both live and consistent.

What this commit does change:

allocate_q gate. padded_heads IS model-wide (get_padded_num_q_heads is a pure
classmethod of the single attention class a process selects, and n_local_heads
depends only on the head count and TP size), so the caller can decide once
whether the buffer can ever be read. On the default SM12x path it cannot: the
FlashInfer-SM120 decode class pads to {16,32,64,128}, so 64/TP maps to itself
and attention takes the in-place branch. That is 256 MiB at TP=2 with
max_num_batched_tokens=8192, against 36 MiB for every aux view in the pool
combined, held for the process lifetime and charged against KV headroom.
q_out() now raises instead of returning None-sliced garbage if the decision was
ever wrong. Do not try to A/B this with the serve-log KV figure -- that is ~1
GiB noisy; the justification is the allocation arithmetic.

Latent dtype-key mismatch, found while auditing: reserve_profile_scratch
reserves under model_config.dtype but _get_q_padded_scratch reads under the
runtime q.dtype, and dtype is part of the cache key. Benign today, but it is an
unproven assumption inside the function the OOM fix depends on, so it is now an
assert rather than silence.

Two test defects, also from the audit:
  - _op_available() gated on the allocating op's name while the helpers now
    call the _out form, so it was proving nothing about what runs.
  - "assert returned.data_ptr() == q_out.data_ptr()" was a tautology: the
    helper returns the very object it was handed. The value check below it is
    the real proof and is kept.

Plus two new tests that had no coverage: the is_valid_token half of
d64074e6f0 (both existing tests pass all-True masks), and a bug-encoding test
